&Q000582 = Letter from Inanaka to the goddess Nintinuga
@composite
#project: etcslatf/inter
#atf: use unicode
#atf: use math
#etcsl: text-id=c.3.3.10
#key: after http://etcsl.orinst.ox.ac.uk/cgi-bin/etcsl.cgi?text=c.3.3.10&display=Crit&charenc=gcirc#

1. {d}nin-tin-ug₅-ga aŋrig zid e₂-kur-ra#
#tr.en: Say to @wPN{Nintinuga}, the reliable stewardess of the E-kur,
#lem: Nintinuga[1]DN; agrig[steward]; zid[right]; Ekur[1]TN
#etcsl: line-id=1

2. a-zu kalam-ma-ra u₃-na-a-dug₄
#tr.en: the physician of the Land; repeat to the lady,
#lem: azu[doctor]; kalam[the Land (of Sumer)]; dug[speak]
#etcsl: line-id=2

3. nin tu₆ dug₄-ga-ni uŋ₃ šar₂ til₃-le
#tr.en: whose incantation heals the multitude of people,
#lem: nin[lady]; tu[incantation]; dug[speak]; uŋ[people]; šar[numerous]V/i; til[live (of a single person)]
#etcsl: line-id=3

4. mu₇-mu₇-ma-ni uŋ₃ sag₉-ga
#tr.en: whose spells make the people recover, to @varh[t3310.v1]{my} @vart[t3310.v2,t3310.v1]{@varn{1 ms. has instead:} the} relenting lady,
#lem: mu[incantation]; uŋ[people]; sag[good]
#etcsl: line-id=4

5. nin šag₄-ne-ša₄ lu₂ til₃-til₃ šudu₃-de₃ ki aŋ₂-ra
#tr.en: who loves to revive the people and loves supplications,
#lem: nin[lady]; šaneša[supplication]; lu[person]; til[live (of a single person)]; šud[prayer]; ki[place]; aŋ[measure]
#etcsl: line-id=5

6. šag₄ gur-ru šag₄-la₂ sug₄ a-ra-zu ŋiš (1:tuku-ŋu₁₀-ur₂:1)
#tr.en: the merciful and compassionate one who listens to prayers.
#lem: šag[heart]; gur[turn]; šala[pity]; sug[full]; arazu[supplication]; ŋeš[tree]; tuku[acquire]
#etcsl: line-id=6

:6v.1.1: ($1 ms. has instead:$) tuku
#lem: tuku[acquire]

7. u₃-na-de₃-tah
#tr.en: You are the caretaker of the living and the dead;
#lem: tah[add]
#etcsl: line-id=7

8. til₃-la ug₅-ga en₃ tar-bi za-e-me-en
#tr.en: you are the great healer of all the crippled ones.
#lem: til[live (of a single person)]; ug[kill]; en[cvne]; tar[cut]; zaʾe[you]
#etcsl: line-id=8

9. lu₂-kud-lu₂-kud-ba du₇-du₇ gal-bi-me-en
#tr.en: This is what @wPN{Inanaka},
#lem: lukud[disabled person]; du[perfect]; gal[big]
#etcsl: line-id=9

10. {m}{d}inana-ka dumu-munus {d}en-lil₂-a₂-mah
#tr.en: the daughter of @wPN{Enlil-a-mah},
#lem: Inanaka[1]PN; dumumunus[daughter]; Enlilamah[1]PN
#etcsl: line-id=10

11. geme₂-zu na-ab-be₂-a
#tr.en: your maidservant says:
#lem: geme[female worker]; e[speak]
#etcsl: line-id=11

12. ki-nu₂ šub-ba-ŋu₁₀-da (1:2-kam-ma-bi:1)
#tr.en: I have fallen ill @varh[t3310.v3]{for a second time} @vart[t3310.v4,t3310.v3]{@varn{1 ms. has instead:} twice (?)}, but I do not yet know the divine oracle concerning @varh[t3310.v5]{my being in agonies} @vart[t3310.v6,t3310.v5]{@varn{1 ms. has instead:} these agonies}. @vart[t3310.v7,t3310.v7]{@varn{1 ms. adds:} …….} My lady,
#lem: kinud[sleeping quarter]; šub[fall]; n
#etcsl: line-id=12

:12v.1.1: ($1 ms. has instead:$) 2-ta-am₃
#lem: n

13. (1:u₂-u₈-a-u₈-a al-ŋen-na-ŋu₁₀ a-ra₂-bi:1) nu-zu
#tr.en: a house has been built for me,
#lem: ua[oh!]; ŋen[go]; ara[way]; zu[know]
#etcsl: line-id=13

:13v.1.1: ($1 ms. has instead:$) u₈-e-a-e a-ra₂-bi
#lem: ua[oh!]; ara[way]

@variants

#etcsl#ms 1 ms. adds:
13A. [...] mu-da-an-TAR [...] RA X [...] X
#lem: X; X; X; X; X; X; X
#etcsl: line-id=13A

@endvariants
14. nin-ŋu₁₀ e₂ ma-an-du₃-ma igi la₂-bi ba-tuš-en
#tr.en: but I have to sit there with longing eyes.
#lem: nin[lady]; e[house]; du[build]; igi[eye]; la[hang]; tuš[sit (down)]
#etcsl: line-id=14

15. zu-a kal-la-ŋu₁₀ ŋiri₃ kur₂ (1:ba-an-dab₅-be₂-eš:1)
#tr.en: My valued acquaintances @varh[t3310.v8]{keep} @vart[t3310.v9,t3310.v8]{@varn{1 ms. has instead:} stay} away from me.
#lem: zu[know]; kal[rare]; ŋiri[foot]; kur[alienate]; dab[seize]
#etcsl: line-id=15

:15v.1.1: ($1 ms. has instead:$) mu-da-an-gub
#lem: gub[stand]

16. lu₂ en₃ tar-re la-ba-an-tuku
#tr.en: I have no one who would take care of me.
#lem: lu[person]; en[cvne]; tar[cut]; tuku[acquire]
#etcsl: line-id=16

17. im-ma-si im-ma-dirig-ga-ta
#tr.en: Since this is full,
#lem: si[fill]; dirig[make more than]
#etcsl: line-id=17

18. zi al-ir-ir-re-en
#tr.en: too full for me, I am distressed.
#lem: zi[life]; ir[cvve]
#etcsl: line-id=18

19. tukum-bi nin-ŋa₂ an-na-kam
#tr.en: If it pleases my lady,
#lem: tukumbi[if]; nin[lady]; anna[approval]
#etcsl: line-id=19

20. a₂-sag₃ su-ŋa₂ ŋal₂-la su-ŋa₂ (1:ib₂-ta-an-zig₃:1)
#tr.en: @varh[t3310.v10]{and the asag demon which is in my body leaves my body,
#lem: Asag[1]DN; su[flesh]; ŋal[⟦be⟧ to be (there]; su[flesh]; zig[rise]
#etcsl: line-id=20

:20v.1.1: ($some mss. have instead:$) he₂-eb-ta-an-zig₃
#lem: zig[rise]

21. (1:ki-in-du:1) nam-til₃-la-ka ŋiri₃-ŋu₁₀ (2:he₂-bi₂-ib-gub-be₂:2)
#tr.en: and thus} @vart[t3310.v11,t3310.v10]{@varn{some mss. have instead:} may the asag demon which is in my body leave my body, so that} @varh[t3310.v12]{it (the asag demon) allows me} @vart[t3310.v13,t3310.v12]{@varn{1 ms. has instead:} I can} step again on the @varh[t3310.v14]{path} @vart[t3310.v15,t3310.v14]{@varn{1 ms. has instead:} ground} of life with my feet.
#lem: kindu[earth]; namtil[life]; ŋiri[foot]; gub[stand]
#etcsl: line-id=21

:21v.1.1: ($1 ms. has instead:$) ki
#lem: ki[place]
:21v.2.1: ($1 ms. has instead:$) he₂-bi₂-ib-gub-be₂-en
#lem: gub[stand]

22. u₃ ŋe₂₆-e (1:geme₂-zu:1) he₂-me-en
#tr.en: @varh[t3310.v16]{I will then be your maidservant,
#lem: u[and]; ŋaʾe[I]; geme[female worker]; me[be]
#etcsl: line-id=22

:22v.1.1: ($some mss. have instead:$) geme₂-ni
#lem: geme[female worker]

23. (1:e₂-za-a:1) kisal-luh-bi he₂-me-en (2:igi-zu-še₃ he₂-gub:2)
#tr.en: the courtyard sweeper of your temple, and will serve you.
#lem: e[house]; kisalluh[courtyard sweeper]; me[be]; igi[eye]; gub[stand]
#etcsl: line-id=23

:23v.1.1: ($some mss. have instead:$) e₂-a-ni
#lem: e[house]
:23v.2.1: ($some mss. have instead:$) igi-ni-še₃ ga-gub
#lem: igi[eye]; gub[stand]

24. u₃-ba-sag₉-ge nin-ŋu₁₀ lu₂-kud-da du₇-du₇
#tr.en: Furthermore after I have recovered, I will name you,
#lem: sag[good]; nin[lady]; lukud[disabled person]; du[perfect]
#etcsl: line-id=24

25. (1:mu-zu ga-am₃-mi-ib₂-sa₄:1)
#tr.en: my lady, as "the healer of the crippled"} @vart[t3310.v17,t3310.v16]{@varn{some mss. have instead:} I will then be her maidservant, the courtyard sweeper of her temple, and will serve her. Furthermore after I have recovered, I will name her, my lady, as "the healer of the crippled"}.
#lem: mu[name]; še[call by name]
#etcsl: line-id=25

:25v.1.1: ($some mss. have instead:$) mu-še₃ ga-sa₄
#lem: mu[name]; še[call by name]







